# Manuscript PDF page 50

                         HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



   I opened the tab again. Then I opened another one, because that’s
how it works, and searched why do I tap my hand when it’s too quiet, and
the algorithm, which has no manners, noticed and offered me the next
thing. A video. A woman in a Honda Civic, a parking ticket on the
dash, an iced coffee going watery in the cup holder. Things nobody told
me were ADHD until I was thirty-four. Four minutes. No music, no
graphics. Just her, talking to a phone, half-laughing at herself.
   I should have scrolled past. I was in the mood to scroll past. I hit
play.
   She started small. She said she could never find her keys, and I
thought everybody. She said she’d been late to her own birthday party,
and I thought everybody. I was settling into the comfortable distance of
this isn’t me, this is just being a person, and then she said:
   “I can’t start anything unless something’s on fire. So I set small fires.
A timer. A friend coming over. A train I have to catch. I thought every‐
body did this. People call it procrastination. I call it infrastructure.”
   I paused it.
   My thumb did it. I was sitting very still, the way you sit when
you’ve heard your name said in a room you thought was empty. The 4
went by the window at that exact second, loud, rattling the glass, and
my mind went to eight-minute sprints, a train at a time, and how I’d
never told anyone, because it sounded like a system and I was embar‐
rassed by anything that worked.
   Other people did this too, and somebody had filed it under a cat‐
egory.
   I let the video go on.
   She said she’d always studied with the TV on, and every teacher
told her mother it meant she wasn’t serious, and it turned out some of
us need noise for the same reason other people need quiet. She said
she’d say five minutes and mean two hours. She said she could tell you
the exact song playing during the worst night of her life but not wheth‐
er she’d taken her keys. She said, near the end, a little lighter, like it was
a small one, “And if somebody’s tone changes, even a little, my whole
day falls through a trapdoor.”




